# Nissan Juke F15 five-door crossover, 2010-2019.
#
# The first Juke: the car that created the small crossover niche in Britain, with
# the split-level lamp arrangement (round main lamps low in the bumper, slim
# lamps along the bonnet edge) and the falling coupe roofline.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nissan_Juke
#
# Dimensions 4.135 x 1.765 x 1.565 m, wheelbase 2.530 m, from the English
# Wikipedia F15 dimension box. Mirrors take the modelled width to 2.008 m.
#
# Colours. No UK colour chart for the F15 was located, so the objects carry plain
# colour names: white, red, black and silver. Weights red 3, white 3, black 2,
# silver 2, total 10, provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 180 km/h for the 1.6, the volume model. Comfort 85/458, on the line
# 330 + 1.5 x 85: a tall, short-wheelbase car, comfortable enough but not a
# long-distance machine. Classes 0,1.
#
# Dates are road presence: September 2010 intro, retires staggered 2025-2028 on
# a fifteen- to eighteen-year tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/nissan-juke-f15.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/nissan-juke-f15.dae (47 objects). References:
# citycars/references/nissan-juke-f15/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the wing bulges are understated and the lower bumpers are
# more angular than the photographs. At 128 px the split-level lamps, the falling
# roofline and the concealed rear door handles read as the Juke.

obj=citycar
name=NissanJukeF15(White)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=3
intro_year=2010
intro_month=9
retire_year=2028
retire_month=6
speed=180
length=3
class=0,1
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=85
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=458

